= Loss aversion =

== Overview ==
'''Loss aversion''' is the hypothesis that, relative to a reference point, losses can have greater psychological impact than commensurate gains.

== Prospect theory ==
Loss aversion is associated with [[Prospect theory]], where outcomes are evaluated as gains or losses relative to a reference point rather than solely as final wealth.

== Scope and debate ==
The magnitude and generality of loss aversion depend on task, stakes, endowment, experience, and measurement. It should not be treated as a universal constant.

== References ==
* Daniel Kahneman & Amos Tversky, “Prospect Theory: An Analysis of Decision under Risk”, ''Econometrica'' 47 (1979): 263–291.
* Tversky & Kahneman, “Loss Aversion in Riskless Choice”, ''Quarterly Journal of Economics'' 106 (1991): 1039–1061.

